# p_3f8a — Skincare split: nightstand weekdays, bathroom shelf weekends (fork of p_b7e2)

Fork of p_b7e2. The mixture's worst-object report shows skincare_elena predicted at nightstand_b1 but actually at bathroom_shelf_ba1 twenty times, e.g. day 4 (Saturday) 08:00. The per-hour data is clear: on weekends the bottle sits at bathroom_shelf_ba1 x2 at every single hour (00:00 through 22:00), while on weekdays it is at nightstand_b1 x3 with bathroom_shelf_ba1 x1 (a 3-to-1 split). The parent document's `class:skincare` block puts everything at bathroom_shelf_ba1, which is correct for weekends and for skincare_priya but wrong for skincare_elena on weekdays. I add a specific `skincare_elena` target that overrides the class: nightstand_b1 on weekdays (chance "usually"), bathroom_shelf_ba1 on weekends (chance "almost_always"). The glasses evening claim (17–21 h nightstand) has resolved for 10 / against 6 overall; the per-hour data at 18:00 (nightstand x3, desk x1) and 20:00 (nightstand x3, coffee_table x1) supports it, so I keep it. I drop the phone_elena claim (for 1, against 3 — too weak to sustain) and replace it with a skincare_elena weekend claim and a glasses weekend nightstand claim.

What sets this apart from p_b7e2: the explicit skincare_elena target (nightstand weekdays / bathroom shelf weekends) and the two new claims testing that split. What would refute it: three consecutive weekend hourly finds of skincare_elena at nightstand_b1, or a weekday find at bathroom_shelf_ba1 three times in a row.

